A||||10||Uaana||State Farm Ins. Outrage||||8||
Z||000000||Uaana||12-08-2000||12:52 AM||||State Farm, the nation's largest automobile insurer, recently announced plans to dump its across-the-board discount for cars equipped with air bags, and will instead be offering staggered rate cuts for vehicles it considers safest, which include big cars, pickup trucks, and some SUVs. Other insurance companies my soon follow with similar plans. Consumer safety experts have criticized the plan, saying it will penalize drivers of smaller vehicles. SUV opponents feel that this encourages a "tank" mentality among SUVs and other big cars, leading these drivers with the impression that they are invulnerable, and disregarding the risk to small cars in crash situations. <P>If you have State Farm you may want to contact your agent.<BR>Mine is trying her best to keep me appraised of any rate hikes I may take because of the purposed policy change.||||reg||8
Z||000001||sparks||12-08-2000||12:41 PM||||Funny, that's a total 180 from where they were a year ago.  A year ago they were jacking up rates on SUV's because they felt that they caused more deaths in the other vehicle involved in the accident rather than saying that they were "safter" for the people driving.<P>Now SUV's should cost less???  Sheesh.  I do have a Jeep, but it's a Wrangler so it'll probably be too small for their definition of a "safe SUV".  Go figure.<P>------------------<BR><BR>Adam Sparks||||reg||8
Z||000002||theogre||12-08-2000||11:16 PM||||re read the last weeks news more carefully.<P>State farm is looking at a discount but at least 2 other major companies are looking at jacking rates on SUV and some vans and trucks.<P>being a state farm customer I'm watching that one carefully.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||12-09-2000||12:09 AM||||Being a 20 year old male, my insurance is high enough. Just few months ago they decided to jack my insurance rate $300 more/year and I hadn't even gotten a ticket/accident etc. My notice to them that it was rediculous was I refused to send payment for a month. When my agent called asking why I missed a payment for the first time in 4 years I told her $300/year more was rediculous and I would change companies before I made the payment. I received a new invoice the next week from her for the "correct" amount, and it has stayed there. When I'm paying almost $1,400/year for coverage as basic as I can get(PL PD), and in college, I'll fight for as much of my money as I can.<P>Like Ogre said, being a State Farm customer I am also watching this one.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||12-09-2000||08:02 AM||||for the first 8 years of driving, i was a state farm customer.  they never hesitated to raise my rates, over the least little thing.<P>when i got my GT, back in'89, they wanted me to pay $750 ever 6 months!<P>after talking with an insurance friend, she tried to cover me but couldnt, she took me to see a friend of her's that was a state farm agent.  <P>this fellow explained that state farm has 6 or 7 different price breaks for each policy/coverage!  he explained that there was no determining factor, other than "the will of the agent", to decide what rate you should be charge!<P>he then wrote me a policy, identical to the one my "family agent" had given me, for 1/2 the cost!  i now owed $375 ever 6 months, not $750!<P>i droped state farm yers ago, and will <B>never</B> use them for anything again!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000005||BAM-BAM||12-09-2000||09:28 AM||||  The insurance buisness is the singal biggest scam on the face of the earth an State Farm in right top of that. Talk about lack of return on your investment, when was the last time anyone ever got anything from their insurance company other than the monthly, semi-anual or anual statement,or rate increse notice ?<BR>   I had State Farm from 18-33, most of that at over $200.00 a month (Trans Am) never got a ticket nor was I involved in a chargable accident. In 1995, my wife was on her way to work when a 10 year old boy jumped off the sidewalk and hit our 1990 Ford Probe. He had been walking alone when someones Rottweiler decided to scale a fence. The kid was startled and jumped back off the curb. It was an unfortunate occurance and thank GOD he only had a concussion and broken collar bone. Witnesses said that my wife did everything humanly possible to avoid the child, it just happened to fast. My wife was not charges or cited and was pretty shaken up. Well, the boys mother sues my insurance and here is how it went down, as stated from our regional State Farm rep. in Scottsdale Az. " It is unfortunate that you had to be involved in this type of situation. We at State Farm understand and feel for all involved. This is a difficult time for the parties involved. After careful consideration of all the data we feel we have no choice but to terminate our agreement with you. Though you bear no fault, State Farm feels it is in the best interest of the parties to settle this claim without litigation. As a reult, due to the amount of settlement and other factors,we feel it is in the best interest of State Farm to teminate our service to you. This will not be effective untill the end of the current policy period.<BR>Sorry for this unfortunate news. Please contact your agent for further assistance".<BR>  Other factors? These guys can be very creative, almost maffia like. Did you know that in 1992 I was involved in a DUI related accident in Reno Nv. that cause over $30,000 in property damage alone, not to mention the medical. Niether did I, I do not drink and haven't been to Reno since 1988. I was involved in a collision on the freway here in Vegas that involved five vehicals. I was #2 in line. A lady stopped on the east bound freeway to look at an accident in the west bound lane, I mean STOPPED!! The vehical infront of me came to a sudden stop and I as well stopped suddenly. The welding rig behind me couldn't stop that fast nor could the pickup behid him. You can pretty much picture it from there. The driver of the welding truck was cited, only because the lady that stopped initialy had left and no one could get here tag#. The driver of the pickup was cited as well for failure to maintain proper following distance. Well, State farm out of the goodness of their heart decided that it was not cost effective to pay the price of litigation to recover damages to my 1988 Grand Am GT. So, they charged me with a "partially at fault accident and paid the repair bill. My agent nor anyone else a State Farm ever relayed this info to me.<BR>  I contacted the regional rep. and chewed some butt. The only thing this guy could say was, that I should pay better attention in the future to the direction of my claims and that I must have been an oversite on the Reno incident. I should periodicaly check with my agent to see that this type of situation doesn't occur again. <BR>  They took it upon themselves to penalize me for things that weren't my fault and then drop me. After years of premiuns that would choke a horse. It's a scam. If you put that kind of money into an investment with no return, are you going to keep it there? They are always looking for angles to raise premiums. Their premium cuts aren't cuts, they're just premiums that don't raise when others do. Better yet, it's against the law not to have insurance. It's flat out legal extortion. You have to pay for something you hopefully never have to use and if you do watch out.<BR> Nough said. I HATE! insurance. <P> B2 OUT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000006||PearlBlueSoul||12-09-2000||02:55 PM||||Yall, don't feel too bad.  I've been in 1 wreck, and have had 1 ticket that resulted in revocation.  I'm with Geico, and check this:  2k every 6 months!  Go figure, eh?  I'm 17 and drive a Deuce.  think that's got something to do with it?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DavidHenshaw||12-10-2000||11:37 PM||||I've been an auto insurance agent for about 25 years and have a different perspective.<P>Auto ins is not an investment. Its an indemnity coverage which is a fancy way of saying its objective is to return the insured to the same financial condition they were in before the accident happened. <P>As for insurance being a racket the industry as a whole shows a profit of about 2% in most years. That's lower than any other industry I've ever read about.<P>To control your cost shop around, raise your deductibles and drive carefully. Face it, without insurance to cover our collective butts none of us would have any assurance of keeping anything that we own; home, car etc.<P>Dave <P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||12-10-2000||11:53 PM||||DaveH, I don't know what state you are operating in but having insurance doesn't guarrantee squat in the few I've lived in. Insurance comapnies regularly deny paying claims and get away with it. By the time you drag them into court or weedle a settlement out of them the only person who got anything was a bunch of lawyers. My father was paying his lawyers fees from sueing an insurance company untill he died in the early 90's some 20 years after the wreck that put him in a wheel chair.<P>Fiero owners beware. the first thing an insurance company will do to an older car is total it when ever posible. Be very sure you understand what that means before accepting those terms. The #1 thing is that the insurance company takes your car. If you buy it back you get a salvage tittle that can make it very hard to put on the road again if it's repairable. Fieros will usually be totaled even if all damage is confined to the replacable body panels.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Black88GT||12-11-2000||12:21 AM||||I have a personal distaste for Auto Insurance company's, and I will tell you why.<P>When I got into a serious accident in my old yellow formula, I figured it was obviously the other womans fault since she ran the stop sign and turned into my lane of traffic.<BR>The speed limit was 30 and I was going 40.  I skidded and hit her.  They said the skid marks indicated I was speeding and due to Marylands new "contributory negligence" law, I was screwed because the other insurance company could prove I was speeding thus they wouldn't have to pay me a dime or at the best 50% of the value of the car.<P>Well, I knew I could make a claim that even if I was going 30 I still would have hit her.<BR>I talked to my fathers lawyer, who told me due to the low book value of my car, even if I won the case I would probably only walk away with 500 bucks at the most after fees are paid, and the trial wouldn't even go to court for 6 months at best.  In addition, he talked to the cop who was biased against me for being young with a sports car, and the cop said he would testify for the old lady!  At the scene, the cop made comments such as "I bet your dad is going to be mad you wrecked his car" among others, which pissed me off since I worked a lot of hours to get my first fiero and to pay insurance/gas.<P>So, after buying the car for 1,800 and putting about 3,500 into it, I walked away with ZERO after this accident, all thanks to the insurance company and Marylands new law, which I am sure had nothing to do with the fact that most of the legislature is in the pockets of these insurance company's after they fund their election bids....<P>And also like the Ogre said, they will total older cars like Fiero's whenever they can, and use THEIR OWN BOOKS to determine the value.  Thats absolute B.S.  When is the government going to smarten up and regulate these company's.  I guess when they stop funding their campaigns.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000010||batboy||12-11-2000||04:52 PM||||Insurance companies get false claims turned in all the time, so they raise the rates to cover these scams. You'd think they'd be more agressive in investigating the false claims, but I guess it's easier to make the good, law abiding citizens pay instead.<P>The State Farm Insurance Company, at least in farm economy based states like Kansas, has higher than average premiums because they spend a great deal of money and profits hiring lawyers and political lobbyists to "protect" the farmers.<P>Farm, crop, and agri-business insurance is State Farm's bread and butter. In reality, they oppose anything remotely resembling environmental regulations because this "costs" the farmer money to comply. (Money they could be buying more insurance with is their thinking.) Basically, State Farm Insurance spends millions of dollars a year to help the farmers and the ag industry to keep polluting the land and water to their little heart's content. Because of this policy, State Farm will never get my business.||||reg||1
